---
// How BrakeNews works and how well it has worked. Every number comes from method.json, written
// by `make export` from the pipeline's own outputs.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CompareBars from "../components/CompareBars.astro";
import method from "../data/method.json";
import { monthLabel, num, percent } from "../lib/format";

const { data, grouping, recall_matching: matching, backtest: bt, model: md, alerts, models } = method;
const alertTotal = alerts ? Object.values(alerts).reduce((s, n) => s + n, 0) : 0;

const FEATURES: Record<string, string> = {
  theme_recalls: "how often this problem has been recalled on other vehicles",
  a: "complaints about the problem in the last 24 months",
  vehicle_age: "the vehicle's age",
  sibling_signals: "signals for the same problem on other model years of the model",
  theme_total: "how common the problem is across all vehicles",
  k3: "complaints in the last 3 months",
  quiet_months: "months since the last complaint",
  make_recalls_5y: "the manufacturer's recalls in the last 5 years",
};
const months = (range: string) =>
  range
    .split(" to ")
    .map((m) => monthLabel(m))
    .join(" to ");
---

<Base
  title="How BrakeNews works, and how well | BrakeNews"
  description="How BrakeNews groups NHTSA owner complaints into problems, flags vehicles before recalls, and how often past flags were followed by a recall."
>
  <h1>How it works, and how well</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    BrakeNews looks for problems owners report about one vehicle far more often than about
    others, before any recall. Here is each step, with how well it did on past data.
  </p>

  <nav class="toc small" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#data">Data</a>
    <a href="#problems">Problems</a>
    <a href="#flags">Flags</a>
    <a href="#backtest">Track record</a>
    <a href="#model">Recall estimate</a>
    <a href="#checks">AI checks and reports</a>
    <a href="#limits">Limits</a>
  </nav>

  <section aria-labelledby="data">
    <h2 id="data">1. The data</h2>
    {data && (
      <p>
        Every vehicle complaint and recall NHTSA publishes: {num(data.complaints)} complaints
        received from {monthLabel(data.first_received)} to {monthLabel(data.last_received)}, and{" "}
        {num(data.recalls ?? 0)} recall campaigns. A complaint is counted once even when it names
        several components. Names, phone numbers, addresses and similar details are masked in the
        complaint text before anything else reads it.
      </p>
    )}
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="problems">
    <h2 id="problems">2. Grouping complaints into problems</h2>
    <p>
      NHTSA files complaints under broad components such as "engine" or "electrical system",
      which mix dozens of unrelated failures. BrakeNews groups complaints by what the owner
      describes instead. Each narrative is turned into a vector of numbers that captures its
      meaning ({models.embedding}), and similar narratives are clustered together.
    </p>
    {grouping && (
      <p>
        That gives {grouping.clusters} groups, holding {percent(grouping.grouped_share)} of the{" "}
        {num(grouping.narratives)} narratives; the rest fit no group well. An AI ({models.labelling})
        named each group from samples, set aside the ones that are not about a defect (for example,
        complaints that recall parts are unavailable) and merged duplicates, leaving{" "}
        {grouping.defect_themes} problems that cover {percent(grouping.defect_share)} of narratives.
      </p>
    )}
    <p>
      The groups are approximate. Similar-sounding complaints can land together: complaints about
      the glare from other drivers' headlights share a group with complaints about dim headlights.
      The checks in step 6 exist for this reason.
    </p>
    {matching && (
      <p>
        Recalls are matched to problems the same way. A recall's defect summary shortlists the
        problems it resembles, and the AI picks the ones it actually covers. It checked{" "}
        {num(matching.checked)} recalls; {percent(matching.matched_share)} cover at least one
        problem. A recall covers a vehicle's problem when it names that make, model and model year.
        Matching misses recalls filed under another spelling of the model (such as "Wrangler 4xe"
        for "Wrangler").
      </p>
    )}
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="flags">
    <h2 id="flags">3. Flagging a vehicle</h2>
    <p>Two tests run every month for every vehicle and problem.</p>
    <h3>Compared with other vehicles</h3>
    <p>
      Over the last 24 months: what share of this vehicle's complaints are about the problem, and
      what share of all other vehicles' complaints are? The ratio of the two is the
      proportional reporting ratio (PRR), the method drug-safety agencies use on adverse-event
      reports. Comparing shares rather than counts cancels what inflates counts without a defect:
      a popular model, an older vehicle, a year with more complaints overall. A vehicle is flagged
      when the problem has at least 3 complaints, the ratio is at least 2, and a chi-square test
      says the difference is unlikely to be chance.
    </p>
    <h3>Compared with its own past</h3>
    <p>
      A surge test asks whether the vehicle's complaints in the last three months have shifted
      toward the problem beyond what its own history predicts (a binomial test, with the false
      discovery rate controlled across all vehicles each month).
    </p>
  </section>

  {bt && (
    <section aria-labelledby="backtest">
      <h2 id="backtest">4. Track record</h2>
      <p>
        The tests were run month by month over the whole history, using only the complaints
        received up to each month, and compared with the recalls that came later.
      </p>
      <dl class="tiles">
        <div class="tile">
          <dt>Recalled problems flagged first</dt>
          <dd class="value num">{percent(bt.flagged_before_recall)}</dd>
          <dd class="note">
            Of {num(bt.recalled_pairs)} vehicle problems later recalled (with 3 or more complaints
            before the recall)
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="tile">
          <dt>How early</dt>
          <dd class="value num">{bt.median_months_ahead} months</dd>
          <dd class="note">Median time from the first flag to the recall</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="tile">
          <dt>Flags followed by a recall</dt>
          <dd class="value num">{percent(bt.signal_followed_by_recall)}</dd>
          <dd class="note">Within 24 months, of {num(bt.signals_judged)} flags old enough to judge</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <CompareBars
        caption="Share of vehicle problems followed by a covering recall within 24 months"
        rows={[
          { label: "Surging (vs its own past)", value: bt.surge_followed_by_recall, text: percent(bt.surge_followed_by_recall) },
          { label: "Flagged (vs other vehicles)", value: bt.signal_followed_by_recall, text: percent(bt.signal_followed_by_recall) },
          { label: "Same number of complaints, never flagged", value: bt.unflagged_followed_by_recall, text: percent(bt.unflagged_followed_by_recall) },
        ]}
      />
      <p>
        A flag made a recall about {bt.lift} times as likely as for problems with as many
        complaints that never stood out; a surge about {bt.surge_lift} times. Most flags are still
        never followed by a recall. Some are not defects, and many real defects are fixed without a
        recall. A flag is a reason to look closer, not a prediction.
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  {md && (
    <section aria-labelledby="model">
      <h2 id="model">5. The recall estimate</h2>
      <p>
        A model (gradient-boosted trees, LightGBM) estimates for each vehicle and problem the
        chance that a recall covering it follows within 24 months. It sees only what was known at
        the time: complaint counts and trends, both tests above, severity (crashes, fires,
        injuries), the vehicle's age, and context such as{" "}
        {md.top_features.slice(0, 4).map((f) => FEATURES[f] ?? f).join(", ")}.
      </p>
      <p>
        It was trained on data from {months(md.train_months)}, tuned on data from{" "}
        {months(md.validation_months)}, and tested on data from {months(md.test_months)}, with gaps between the periods so no recall in one
        period leaks into another. On the test period, at the same number of alerts as the PRR
        flag ({num(md.rule_alerts)}):
      </p>
      <CompareBars
        caption={`Of the vehicle problems recalled in the test period, the share each method alerted on (same number of alerts)`}
        rows={[
          { label: "Model", value: md.model_recall, text: percent(md.model_recall) },
          { label: "PRR flag", value: md.rule_recall, text: percent(md.rule_recall) },
        ]}
      />
      <CompareBars
        caption="Of the alerts, the share followed by a covering recall within 24 months"
        rows={[
          { label: "Model", value: md.model_precision, text: percent(md.model_precision) },
          { label: "PRR flag", value: md.rule_precision, text: percent(md.rule_precision) },
          { label: "Any vehicle problem (base rate)", value: md.base_rate, text: percent(md.base_rate) },
        ]}
      />
      <p class="small muted">
        Ranking quality on the test period: average precision {md.average_precision} (the PRR
        ratio alone: {md.prr_average_precision}), ROC AUC {md.roc_auc}. The estimates run slightly
        low: they average {percent(md.mean_score)} against {percent(md.base_rate)} observed.
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  <section aria-labelledby="checks">
    <h2 id="checks">6. AI checks and investigation reports</h2>
    {alerts && (
      <p>
        Because the problem groups are approximate, an AI ({models.labelling}) reads the recent
        complaints behind each of the {num(alertTotal)} highest-ranked alerts and decides, one
        complaint at a time, whether it is about that problem on that vehicle. {num(alerts.confirmed ?? 0)}{" "}
        alerts were confirmed, {num(alerts.mixed ?? 0)} were mixed and {num(alerts.not_confirmed ?? 0)}{" "}
        were not confirmed. Vehicle pages flag a problem only when its alert was confirmed and no
        recall covers it yet.
      </p>
    )}
    <p>
      For the top confirmed alerts, an AI investigator ({models.investigator}) reads the
      complaints, compares other model years and vehicles, looks up recalls, and writes a report
      that cites the NHTSA complaint numbers behind each finding. A second AI ({models.reviewer})
      fact-checks every finding against the full text of the complaints it cites, and the
      investigator revises if needed. Only reports the reviewer approves are published. No person
      reviews them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="limits">
    <h2 id="limits">7. Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Complaints are written by owners and are not verified by NHTSA or by BrakeNews.</li>
      <li>
        A recall is only one outcome of a real defect, and many recalls follow a manufacturer's own
        findings rather than complaints. "Followed by a recall" undercounts real problems.
      </li>
      <li>Problem groups, recall matches and checks are made by AI and can be wrong.</li>
      <li>Vehicles with fewer than 25 complaints have no page; their numbers are too small to compare.</li>
      <li>
        Nothing here is safety advice. Check your vehicle's open recalls with your VIN at{" "}
        <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/recalls" rel="noopener">nhtsa.gov/recalls</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small muted">
      Source code: <a href="https://github.com/Hariswar/BrakeNews" rel="noopener">github.com/Hariswar/BrakeNews</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .toc {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding: var(--space-3) 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .toc a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 36px;
    padding: 0 12px;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .toc a:hover {
    border-color: var(--link);
  }
  section li {
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }
</style>
